e":"Mid Suffolk Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Babergh Council","time":"5.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Babergh housing scheme faces \u00a34.9m loss with units unsold","lead":"Babergh District Council faces \u00a34.889m in expected losses\u2014money it no longer expects to recover\u2014on its Hadleigh housing development scheme, financed through \u00a312.8m in loans. Although the scheme has achieved practical completion, not all housing units have been sold. The council has withdrawn \u00a3938k from its reserves to cover anticipated losses, with the total loss unknowable until later in 2026/27. Babergh is not alone in struggling with commercial housing ventures. Mid Suffolk's property scheme cancelled its second development phase after writing off \u00a3877k in costs, then sold the phase two land for \u00a32.7m. Both councils' commercial housing initiatives have failed to meet financial expectations, compounding financial strain as Babergh's debt service costs have risen to 33 per cent of revenue, above the 28 per cent budgeted.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Babergh and Mid Suffolk face \u00a34.9m loan loss as housing ventures falter","lead":"Babergh and Mid Suffolk councils are writing off nearly \u00a35m on failed housing and commercial property ventures, with external auditors flagging concerns about their financial controls. Babergh Growth Ltd's housing scheme at a former council office is expected to lose \u00a34.889m\u2014an increase from \u00a33.1m in one year\u2014because units are not selling. Mid Suffolk scrapped a second housing development, writing off \u00a3877k in costs, though the land later sold for \u00a32.7m. Both councils have also recognised \u00a3434k expected credit loss each on \u00a343.3m loans to CIFCO Ltd, a commercial property investment company that is deferring interest payments. The councils are additionally struggling to deliver infrastructure projects: Mid Suffolk has \u00a312m in unspent Community Infrastructure Levy funds allocated since 2018/19, with walking and cycling projects achieving just 0.7% spend despite allocating \u00a33.3m to 13 schemes. External auditors have recommended the Statement of Accounts process be subject to ongoing monitoring.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"BC/26/19 - CIL Expenditure Framework Review 8","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43635/43635.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Babergh and Mid Suffolk sit on \u00a313m unspent community funds as delivery lags","lead":"Babergh and Mid Suffolk councils are holding \u00a313 million in community infrastructure funds (money set aside from new developments to pay for local projects) that has not yet been spent, with delivery of walking and cycling schemes particularly stalled. Mid Suffolk has allocated \u00a33.3 million for 13 walking and cycling projects but spent only \u00a323,905 as of March 2026. The councils' own review, approved this week, extends normal project completion timelines from two years to three years and raises officer approval limits from \u00a330,000 to \u00a350,000, suggesting recognition that infrastructure schemes are taking significantly longer to deliver than originally planned. The backlog grew sharply last year, when \u00a311 million was allocated but only \u00a3695,000 spent.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"BC/26/19 - CIL Expenditure Framework Review 8","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43635/43635.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Mid Suffolk Council","paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","headline":"Significant external economic uncertainty: Middle East war triggered commodity price spike and upward pressure on interest rates.","detail":"The financial year included the US/Israel-Iran war from February 2026, causing oil and commodity prices to spike as shipping lanes closed. PWLB 10-year certainty rates rose from 5.38% to 5.72% by year-end. The Bank of England held rates at 3.75% in March but signalled potential moves in either direction, with financial markets pricing in rate hikes. Economic outlook described as highly uncertain.","category":"other","matters":"Geopolitical shocks create material uncertainty around future inflation, interest rates, and growth, directly affecting councils' future borrowing costs and capital programme affordability.","figures":"PWLB 10-year rate: 5.38% to 5.72%; UK CPI February 2026: 3.0%; Bank Rate: 3.75%; GDP Q4 2025: +0.1%","quote":"The end of February 2026 saw the start of the war between US/Israel and Iran. The conflict caused oil and other commodity prices to rise sharply as the shipping lanes in the region became effectively closed, restricting global oil supply.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Mid Suffolk Council","paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","headline":"CIFCO commercial investment portfolio (\u00a343.3m loans per council) shows \u00a3434k expected credit loss and \u00a31.3m cumulative deferred interest.","detail":"Both councils have \u00a343.3m loans outstanding to CIFCO Ltd, their main subsidiary for generating investment income. Expected credit losses of \u00a3434k were calculated by the treasury advisers (Arlingclose) at 31 March 2026. CIFCO is paying interest in arrears with \u00a31.3m cumulative deferrals, and the business plan permits a further 10% interest deferment.","category":"income","matters":"Primary income-generating vehicle showing stress through credit loss recognition and deferred interest; weak cash generation from commercial property portfolio threatens expected investment income streams.","figures":"\u00a343.3m loans to CIFCO Ltd per council; \u00a3434k ECL; \u00a31.3m cumulative deferred interest; \u00a313.9m investment income generated to date","quote":"As at 31 March 2026, Arlingclose the Councils treasury advisors have calculated the expected credit loss (ECL) to each Council on the loans made to CIFCO Ltd was \u00a3434k.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Mid Suffolk Council","paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","headline":"Both councils' commercial housing schemes underperformed: Babergh faces growing losses, Mid Suffolk cancelled phase 2.","detail":"Babergh Growth (Hadleigh) has \u00a34.889m expected credit loss with practical completion but poor sales. Mid Suffolk Growth (Needham Market) cancelled phase 2 entirely, writing off \u00a3877k in costs, then sold the phase 2 land for \u00a32.7m. Both ventures in the housing development market have failed to meet financial expectations.","category":"capital","matters":"Pattern of underperformance on commercial housing schemes across both councils suggests either weak market conditions or poor project appraisal capability; raises concerns about ability to deliver future complex capital schemes.","figures":"Babergh: \u00a34.889m ECL. Mid Suffolk: \u00a3877k phase 2 write-off; phase 2 land sold for \u00a32.7m","quote":"Phase two of the development has been cancelled, having incurred costs of \u00a3877k which were written off in the 2025/26 Statement of Accounts.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Mid Suffolk Council","paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","headline":"Mid Suffolk held \u00a34m LOBO loans but lender did not exercise rate-increase option during 2025/26.","detail":"Mid Suffolk continues to hold \u00a34m LOBO loans from Dexia Credit Local where the lender has contractual rights to propose rate increases on 22 February and 22 August annually, with the council able to accept or repay at par. The lender did not exercise this option in 2025/26, avoiding a potential refinancing event at a time when PWLB rates rose 34 basis points.","category":"borrowing","matters":"LOBO loans create future refinancing risk and potential liquidity demand on call dates; non-exercise this year is positive but the exposure remains and creates uncertainty in cash planning.","figures":"\u00a34m LOBO loans outstanding; average rate 4.21%; call dates February and August each year","quote":"The lender has not exercised its option during 2025/26.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Mid Suffolk Council","paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","headline":"Mid Suffolk reduced total borrowing by \u00a314.169m (largely \u00a313m reduction in expensive short-term debt).","detail":"Mid Suffolk delivered significant deleveraging, cutting total borrowing from \u00a3106.955m to \u00a392.786m, with net borrowing falling from \u00a337.288m to \u00a325.188m. The reduction was predominantly in short-term borrowing (\u00a318m to \u00a35m), which had risen to 5.0-7.0% rates by year-end. This material shift away from high-cost short-term funding reduces exposure to volatile rates.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Rapid significant deleveraging changes the council's risk profile and may indicate either constrained cash position or deliberate strategy to reduce financial risk; worth confirming is sustainable.","figures":"Total borrowing decreased \u00a314.169m; short-term borrowing decreased \u00a313m; net borrowing decreased \u00a312.1m","quote":"As at 31 March 2026 Babergh held \u00a3123.6m of loans, a decrease of \u00a32m and Mid Suffolk held \u00a392.7m of loans, a decrease of \u00a314.2m since 31 March 2025.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Babergh","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":false,"headline":"Significant external economic uncertainty: Middle East war triggered commodity price spike and upward pressure on interest rates.","lead":"Geopolitical shocks create material uncertainty around future inflation, interest rates, and growth, directly affecting councils' future borrowing costs and capital programme affordability.","also":["76 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Joint Audit and Standards Committee on 2026-09-28","n_findings":77,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"MC/26/15 Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","top_paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43661/43661.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":167,"news_drafts_total":3}}